{"reference":"Matthew 20","book":"Matthew","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Service, Wages, and Ransom","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The vineyard workers and their wages belong to the unequal agrarian economy reflected throughout Matthew’s world, with estates, laborers, patrons, and hired work. That setting makes the dispute over equal pay concrete rather than abstract. Later, Jesus joins the Son of Man’s service, the giving of his life, and “a ransom for many” in one statement (v. 28); the precise way these terms relate has been debated.","verse_refs":["Matthew 20:1-16","Matthew 20:20-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_020_194d80dcd47adac078de92f7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}